From: Stephen Bates <sbates@raithlin.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 21:32:59 -0500
Subject: [RFC PATCH 1/8] ionic: run the netdev ops under the instance lock

The queue management API, which a later patch implements, makes the core
call a driver's ndos and ethtool ops with the netdev instance lock held,
and so does request_ops_lock. ionic can't be called that way today:
napi_enable(), napi_disable(), netif_napi_add() and netif_napi_del()
take that lock themselves, and ionic calls them from ndo_open, ndo_stop,
ndo_change_mtu, ndo_bpf, ndo_hwtstamp_set and the ring and channel
ethtool ops. The first "ip link set up" would deadlock.

Set request_ops_lock, and make the instance lock the outer lock for
everything that brings queues up or down:

 - Use the _locked NAPI helpers in the functions that are now only
   reached with the lock held: ionic_qcq_enable(), ionic_qcq_disable(),
   ionic_lif_qcq_deinit(), ionic_lif_txq_init(), ionic_lif_rxq_init()
   and ionic_lif_adminq_init().

 - Take the lock, before lif->queue_lock, in the paths that reach those
   functions from outside the core: the link check (which runs from the
   deferred work, from ionic_lif_register() and at the end of
   ionic_restart_lif()), the Tx timeout work, FW down and FW up
   (ionic_restart_lif(), also the PCI reset_done handler), the hwstamp
   replay after FW up, and the PCI reset_prepare handler.

 - The Tx timeout work is not cancelled by a firmware or PCI reset, so
   it can be waiting for the lock while the reset frees the queues.
   Have it look at netif_running() and IONIC_LIF_F_FW_RESET once it
   holds the lock, where both stand still, rather than before.

 - The adminq NAPI is added and enabled by ionic_lif_init() and removed
   by ionic_lif_deinit(), which run both from probe and remove, without
   the lock, and from the FW recovery paths, which now hold it. Split
   each in two: ionic_lif_init() and ionic_lif_deinit() take the lock for
   probe and remove, and the _locked variants are for the recovery paths.

 - ionic_lif_deinit() cancels the deferred work and the Tx timeout work.
   Both now take the instance lock, so that can't be done with the lock
   held. Only the callers without IONIC_LIF_F_FW_RESET get as far as the
   cancel, and those are probe and remove, so the cancel stays in the
   unlocked ionic_lif_deinit(), ahead of the lock.

The lock order is the instance lock, then lif->queue_lock, then the
locks that were already taken inside queue_lock. Registering and
unregistering the auxiliary device stay outside the instance lock, as
they are outside queue_lock today: the RDMA driver's probe and remove
run from there.

For an ops locked driver the ethtool ops run without rtnl_lock unless
the driver asks for it. get_link needs it: ethtool_op_get_link() syncs
the link watch, see commit 1105ef941c1a ("net: ethtool: keep rtnl_lock
for ops using ethtool_op_get_link()"). Ask for it for set_ringparam and
set_channels as well. They rebuild the queues and, for the channels,
change the number of real queues, and keeping rtnl_lock there is the
cautious choice, which gve, bnxt and fbnic made too. The other ops only
talk to the device and touch driver state that the instance lock now
covers.

The core refuses to register an ops locked netdev that still has
ndo_set_rx_mode ("ops-locked drivers should use ndo_set_rx_mode_async"),
so convert it: the async op runs in process context under the instance
lock with snapshots of the address lists, which lets it sync the filters
and the rx_mode to the device directly instead of through the deferred
work.

This follows what bnxt did before it could use the queue API, in
commit 004b5008016a ("eth: bnxt: remove most dependencies on RTNL") and
commit eaca6e5dc6ba ("eth: bnxt: request unconditional ops lock").
